{
  "title": "OpenEMR \u2014 healthcare (5,153\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: OpenEMR\nDomain: healthcare | Scale: large | Stars: 5,153\nProblem: OpenEMR provides a free, open-source electronic health records (EHR) and medical practice management solution for healthcare providers. It addresses the need for affordable, customizable healthcare IT infrastructure that can be deployed across multiple platforms globally.\nTarget users: Healthcare providers and clinics, Medical practices, Hospitals and health systems, International healthcare organizations, Healthcare IT developers and integrators\nCore features: Electronic Health Records (EHR) management; Medical practice management and scheduling; Electronic billing and claims processing; FHIR API and interoperability support; Multi-language and internationalization support; RESTful API for integration\nIntegrations: FHIR (Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources), ONC Certification compliance, Inferno Certification testing, Docker and containerization, Third-party healthcare billing systems\nTech stack: JavaScript, jQuery, CSS/SCSS, Twig templating, HTML5 + PHP with Composer package management + MySQL/MariaDB (inferred from healthcare requirements) + Docker, cross-platform (Windows, Linux, macOS)\nKey libraries: Gulp (build automation), PostCSS and Autoprefixer (CSS processing), ESLint (JavaScript linting), Jest (JavaScript testing), Stylelint (CSS/SCSS linting), PHPStan (static analysis), XSLT (data transformation)\nArchitecture: monolith\nDesign lessons:\n- Enterprise healthcare systems require extensive CI/CD pipelines with compliance testing (Inferno certification, PHPStan analysis, isolated test suites) to ensure patient data safety and regulatory compliance.\n- Multi-language and cross-platform support demands careful build tooling\u2014OpenEMR uses Gulp, npm, and Composer to manage frontend and backend asset compilation separately.\n- FHIR interoperability is critical for modern EHR systems; OpenEMR prioritizes API-first design alongside legacy EHR functionality to enable healthcare data exchange.\n- Large open-source healthcare projects benefit from strong community governance, documented security vulnerability processes, and professional/volunteer contribution frameworks to maintain trust and sustainability.",
